        <!--
            Engine and APU fire visuals (visual layer only, nothing here feeds back into the systems).
            - Driven by the fire itself, not by the detection, and by one signal only: L:A32NX_ENG_1(2)_ON_FIRE /
              L:A32NX_APU_ON_FIRE, "the zone is burning", written by systems.wasm (SetOnFireModule, fire_protection.rs):
              set by the flyPad fire failure or by the sim's own fire of the MSFS failures menu (ENG ON FIRE:1(2), APU ON
              FIRE DETECTED), cleared when the failure is removed / the sim fire is cleared, or when an AGENT discharge
              puts the fire out (1/2 chance per bottle). The sim variables are not read here: they do not go off when an
              agent puts the fire out, and kept the effects on. A FIRE TEST shows nothing: it lights the warnings only,
              there is no fire (chime, MASTER WARNING, ECAM, FIRE panel and ENG
              MASTER panel lights).
            - The effect stays on after the engine shutdown, until the fire is out (the FIRE pb does not put a fire out).
            - Every effect is ANDed with L:A32NX_CONFIG_SITUATION_FX, the flyPad Settings > Sim Options switch "Situation
              Visual Effects" (default on), written by the flyPad from its persistent setting CONFIG_SITUATION_FX.
            - Looks (design choice, how a fire looks is not documented): two stock MSFS 2024 visual effect
              graphs of the package fs-base-effects (VisualEffectLibs/Asobo/Base), FX_Fire_1_Small (flames) and
              FX_Smoke_Fire (smoke: flames alone looked bare) at each engine exhaust node and FX_Smoke_Fire (smoke) at the APU exhaust node (EngineSmoke spread as a general haze around the aircraft in the sim). Their GUIDs are the ones stored in
              the .spb files, where the SDK template GUIDs (contrails, heat, ballast) are found too. No offset: FX
              offsets proved unreliable on the A380X jettison plume.
            - aircraft.cfg [EFFECTS] lines with the legacy fire effects (fx_engfire, FX_ASOBO_ENGINEFIRE_SMALL) showed
              nothing in MSFS 2024 (seen in the sim); there, EngineFire = fx_dummy turns the sim's own fire effect
              off so that a fire does not show twice.
            - The engine effects hang on FX_ENGINE_FIRE_LEFT/RIGHT, full-scale markers (model fx_engine_fire) at the
              position of fx_exhaust_left/right: those are scaled 0.5 by models.json, which drew the fire at half the size
              of the A380X one (seen in the sim).
            - EngineFireEffects.spec.ts (src/behavior) checks these components.
        -->
        <Component ID="ENGINE_FIRE_FX">
            <Component ID="ENGINE_FIRE_FX_1" Node="FX_ENGINE_FIRE_LEFT">
                <UseTemplate Name="ASOBO_GT_FX">
                    <FX_GUID>{75A93861-D48A-456B-BCA5-7C3AFF31C0F6}</FX_GUID>
                    <FX_NAME>FX_ENGINE_FIRE_1</FX_NAME>
                    <FX_CODE>(L:A32NX_ENG_1_ON_FIRE, bool) (L:A32NX_CONFIG_SITUATION_FX, bool) and</FX_CODE>
                </UseTemplate>
            </Component>
            <Component ID="ENGINE_FIRE_SMOKE_FX_1" Node="FX_ENGINE_FIRE_LEFT">
                <UseTemplate Name="ASOBO_GT_FX">
                    <FX_GUID>{15B35478-6321-4480-8642-EB44248318E2}</FX_GUID>
                    <FX_NAME>FX_ENGINE_FIRE_SMOKE_1</FX_NAME>
                    <FX_CODE>(L:A32NX_ENG_1_ON_FIRE, bool) (L:A32NX_CONFIG_SITUATION_FX, bool) and</FX_CODE>
                </UseTemplate>
            </Component>
            <Component ID="ENGINE_FIRE_FX_2" Node="FX_ENGINE_FIRE_RIGHT">
                <UseTemplate Name="ASOBO_GT_FX">
                    <FX_GUID>{75A93861-D48A-456B-BCA5-7C3AFF31C0F6}</FX_GUID>
